00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,160
Chapter 11. V as in victory. P, B, F, and V are all popped. When pronounced

2
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:15,420
correctly, V shouldn't stand out too much. Its sound, although noticeable, is small.

3
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:20,940
As a result, people depending on their native language sometimes confuse V with

4
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:29,080
B, Spanish, Japanese, with F, German, or with W, Chinese, Hindi. These four sounds

5
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:34,500
are not at all interchangeable. The W is a semivowel and there is no friction or

6
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:42,040
contact. The B, like P, uses both lips and has a slight pop. Americans tend to have a

7
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:47,680
strong popping P. You can check your pronunciation by holding a match, a sheet

8
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:52,580
of paper, or just your hand in front of your mouth. If the flame goes out, the

9
00:00:52,580 --> 00:00:58,160
paper wavers, or you feel a distinct puff of air on your hand, you've said P, not B.

10
00:00:58,820 --> 00:01:05,260
B is the voice pair of P. Although F and V are in exactly the same position, F is a

11
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:12,140
hiss and V is a buzz. The V is the voice pair of F. When you say F, it's as if

12
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:18,840
you're whispering. So for V, say F and simply add some voice to it, V, which is

13
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:23,860
the whole difference between fairy and very, as you'll hear in our next exercise.

14
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:32,160
The F, too, presents problems to Japanese who say hh. To pronounce ff, the lower lip

15
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:36,820
raises up and the inside of the lip very lightly touches the outside of the upper

16
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:41,540
teeth, and you make a slight hissing sound. Don't bite the outside of your lip

17
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:41,940
at all.
